The driver provides support for the TSL2560 and TSL2561 light‐to‐
digital  converter with the API.  The TSL2560 is designed to work
with SMBus at 100 kHz.  The TSL2561 is designed to work with  I2C
Fast‐Mode at 400 kHz.  The sensors come in a variety of packages,
including  6‐lead  Chipscale  6‐lead  TMB  dual  flat no‐lead and
6‐lead ChipLED The package requires a different  set  of  coeffi‐
cients  for  calculating  the Lux value from the raw sensor data.
This behavior is enabled by specifying the  flag  in  the  kernel
configuration file or by using a variable; see below.  The driver
exports  some variables to control the behavior of the sensor and
driver: This variable indicates if the driver instance  has  been
configured  to  use  the coeffecients appropriate for the package
variant.  This variable indicates if the driver has been  config‐
ured  to use an auto‐gain algorithm to improve sensitivity of the
sensor while taking care to avoid sensor  saturation.   Auto‐gain
is  disabled  by  default.   This variable indicates the selected
sensor gain.  If auto‐gain is enabled, this will reflect the cur‐
rent gain setting selected by the  auto‐gain  algorithm.   Other‐
wise,  it  reflects the previously‐configured gain.  Valid values
are and The default gain is Writing to this  variable  implicitly
disables auto‐gain.  This variable indicates the selected analog‐
to‐digital  converter integration time.  Longer integration times
correspond to more accurate readings, at the cost of more  costly
read  operation.   Valid values are and The default value is Note
that that due to the granularity of sleep timing in  the  kernel,
the  driver will busy‐wait for wait times less than 1 Hz, and add
an additional sleep clock tick for wait times greater than 1  Hz.
